# Persistent per-player hands

Add the Cards in Hand sheet, persistent per-player resource counts, and shared one-card stack removal behavior.

## Acceptance criteria

- [x] The middle Hand action from TASK-023 opens a large bottom sheet titled `Cards in hand`.
- [x] The sheet has an obvious Close action and no Save or Cancel actions.
- [x] The current shared player selector appears at the top and switches immediately between six independent hands.
- [x] An unassigned player hand starts empty.
- [x] Brick, Wood, Hay, Sheep, and Ore use the shared card visuals and appear in that order.
- [x] Tapping a resource adds exactly one card to the selected player's hand and autosaves immediately.
- [x] Duplicate resources render as the existing staggered, white-outlined stack.
- [x] Tapping a selected stack removes exactly one card; removing the last copy removes the stack.
- [x] Clear removes every card only from the selected player's hand and autosaves immediately.
- [x] Closing and reopening the sheet preserves every player's hand.
- [x] Per-player hands are value-based domain state independent of SwiftUI and serialize with the Board/game state.
- [x] Decoding older Board data without hands produces six empty hands without losing tiles, pieces, or ownership.
- [x] Hand mutations do not alter another player's hand, the Board layout, or custom-plan card targets.
- [x] Plan Browser inputs refresh from the latest hand for the selected player; switching players uses that player's hand and does not retain stale values.
- [x] Cards-plan selected stacks use the same tap-to-remove-one interaction, but remain draft-only until Save and revert on Cancel.
- [x] Shared add/remove/clear behavior is factored so the Hand and Cards-plan editors cannot diverge unnecessarily.
- [x] Domain tests cover add, remove, final removal, Clear, player isolation, serialization, and legacy decoding.
- [x] UI regression coverage compiles for sheet entry, autosave/reopen, player switching, and plan-draft removal.
- [ ] Focused UI checks pass on a functioning simulator.

## Scope notes

- Resource cards only are in scope; development cards and hidden-information policy are not specified.
- Persist hands at the same boundary as the current Board/game state. Designing a separate account or cloud store is out of scope.
- Plan simulation mathematics remain outside this task; the task must expose and propagate current-hand inputs so the existing or future statistics provider can recompute correctly.

## Verification

- Prefer a serializable `[PlayerColor: ResourceHand]`-style value model with mutation APIs that discard zero/negative counts.
- Record the exact build/test commands, legacy fixture coverage, and remaining visual checks before review.

